#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Refuse a commit that stages a real device id.
#
# WHY A HOOK AND NOT A RULE. Two instances on 2026-08-31, hours apart, by someone
# who had just written the rule down: an example annotation carrying a real id
# cost an hour of alarm mid-incident, and then a `git add -A` swept an operator
# scratch file into a public repo. A CLAUDE.md entry does not survive the
# twentieth minute of a good debugging run. `git add -A` stages what you did not
# look at, and that is the actual mechanism.
#
# WHAT IT MATCHES. A bare 16-hex-digit token, which is the device-id shape
# (efuse MAC derived). Word boundaries mean a 40-char git SHA does not match --
# there is no boundary inside a longer hex run -- and neither does 0xdeadbeef...
# for the same reason.
#
# ALLOWLIST. The obviously-fake ones the docs are supposed to use, plus the
# synthetic bench identity, which is a real enrolled id but is synthetic BY
# DESIGN and exists precisely so that real ids never have to appear.
set -u

# ONE ALLOWLIST, SHARED WITH scripts/check_device_ids.py. They were separate
# inline lists for about four minutes on 2026-08-31 and this hook promptly
# refused a commit the CI check considered clean. Two guards on one rule is two
# rules, and the second one is always the stale one.
ALLOWFILE='scripts/device-id-allowlist.txt'
ALLOW=$(sed 's/#.*//' "$ALLOWFILE" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' 	' | grep -E '^[0-9a-f]{16}$' | paste -sd'|' -)
[ -z "$ALLOW" ] && { echo "pre-commit: cannot read $ALLOWFILE -- refusing to run blind" >&2; exit 1; }
PATTERN='\b[0-9a-f]{16}\b'
found=0

while IFS= read -r f; do
  [ -z "$f" ] || [ ! -e "$f" ] && continue
  # Read the STAGED content, not the worktree: they differ under `git add -p`,
  # and the staged bytes are what the commit will contain.
  hits=$(git show ":$f" 2>/dev/null | grep -oE "$PATTERN" | grep -vE "^($ALLOW)$" | sort -u)
  if [ -n "$hits" ]; then
    found=1
    echo "  $f"
    echo "$hits" | sed 's/^/      /'
  fi
done < <(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM)

if [ "$found" -ne 0 ]; then
  cat >&2 <<'MSG'

COMMIT REFUSED: a real-looking device id is staged.

A device id is not a credential -- the key is HMAC(DEVICE_KEY_SECRET, id) and the
secret does the work -- but a real id in the tree becomes a record somebody acts
on later, and once ids belong to customers it is a privacy surface too.

Use an obviously-fake id and label it:

    # EXAMPLE -- not a real device
    0000000000000000

Allowed: 0000000000000000, 0123456789abcdef, beefbeefbeefbeef (synthetic).

If a real id genuinely belongs in this commit, say so deliberately:

    git commit --no-verify

MSG
  exit 1
fi
exit 0
